{
  "abstract": "Markman's proof of the Hodge conjecture for split Weil sixfolds uses an equivariant object whose equivariant Ext algebra is generated by Hochschild cohomology (condition (M)). rStation paper hodge_conjecture_131 showed that (M) forces $\\chi(F,F)=|G|\\,e(\\mathrm{ch}\\,F)$. This rules (M) out for one-place secant classes when $4\\mid g$, and forces $\\chi(F,F)=-2|G|$ when $g\\equiv2\\bmod4$. We treat the second case on a principally polarized abelian $g$-fold $(X,\\Theta)$, for every imaginary quadratic Weil field $K=\\mathbf Q(\\sqrt{-q})$ and every rational B-field. A class there is $v=\\mathrm{Re}(\\lambda e^{(a+i\\sqrt q)\\Theta})$, and (M) forces $|G|=(4q)^{g/2}|\\lambda|^2/4$. Perry described the invariant category $D(X)^G$ as a twisted derived category of an isogenous abelian variety $N$. Using this and Hotchkiss's local description of the equivalence, we show that Chern characters are transported by a ring isomorphism times $\\tau_0e^{\\sigma}$, where $\\tau_0$ is a positive integer. Integrality of B-field-invariant polynomials on $N$ then gives the following for $g\\ge6$, on every such $X$ whatever its Néron–Severi group. (A) (M) forces $4\\tau_0^2\\mid|\\lambda|^2$; the obstruction is the integrality of $(\\Delta/2)^{g/2}/(g/2)!$ for the discriminant $\\Delta$. (B) (M) forces $4d\\tau_0^2\\mid|\\lambda|^2$, where $d$ is the squarefree part of $q$; this adds the content of the square of a primitive polarization on $N$. (C) For $g=6$ and $K=\\mathbf Q(\\sqrt{-3})$, where (B) says $9\\mid\\mathrm{Nm}$, an exhaustive enumeration under Picard number $1$ also excludes all classes of norm $9$, the classes $6v_1$ and $12v_1$, and two classes of norm $63$. No Hodge class changes status.",
